<h2 id="install">Install</h2>
<p>
    There are two ways. The .NET tool is easiest to keep up to date; the standalone binary needs nothing else
    installed. Both are the same native program.
</p>

<h3 id="install-tool">As a .NET tool</h3>
<ol class="steps-list">
    <li>
        <strong>Install the .NET 10 SDK</strong>, if <code>dotnet --version</code> doesn't already print 10 or later.
        <CodeBlock Title="Windows (PowerShell)" Text="winget install Microsoft.DotNet.SDK.10" />
        <CodeBlock Title="macOS (Homebrew)" Text="brew install --cask dotnet-sdk" />
        <CodeBlock Title="Ubuntu" Text="sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y dotnet-sdk-10.0" />
        Other systems: <a href="@Links.DotNet">dotnet.microsoft.com</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Install DbnFetch</strong> from NuGet, where it is published as <a href="@Links.NuGetFetch">DbnFetch</a>.
        NuGet picks the native build for your system. <code>dbnf tape</code> needs version 0.2.0 or later; update an
        older install with <code>dotnet tool update -g DbnFetch</code>.
        <CodeBlock Title="Any terminal" Text="dotnet tool install -g DbnFetch" />
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>On macOS and Linux, put .NET tools on your PATH</strong> if this is your first one. Add this line to
        <code>~/.zshrc</code> (macOS) or <code>~/.bashrc</code> (Linux), then open a new terminal. Windows does this
        for you.
        <CodeBlock Title="~/.zshrc or ~/.bashrc" Text="@ToolsPath" />
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Check it runs.</strong>
        <CodeBlock Title="Any terminal" Text="dbnf --version" />
    </li>
</ol>
<p>Update with <code>dotnet tool update -g DbnFetch</code>; remove with <code>dotnet tool uninstall -g DbnFetch</code>.</p>

<h3 id="install-binary">As a standalone binary</h3>
<p>
    Each <a href="@Links.Releases">release</a> has a zip of the <code>dbnf</code> binary for
    <code>win-x64</code>, <code>win-arm64</code>, <code>osx-arm64</code> (Apple silicon) and <code>linux-x64</code>.
    Unzip it into a folder on your PATH.
</p>
<CodeBlock Title="Windows (PowerShell)" Text="@BinaryWindows" />
<CodeBlock Title="macOS" Text="@BinaryMac" />
<CodeBlock Title="Linux" Text="@BinaryLinux" />
<p>On Windows, open a new terminal after changing PATH. On macOS, <code>xattr</code> clears the download quarantine that would otherwise block an unsigned binary.</p>

<h2 id="login">Log in to an archive</h2>
<p>
    You need the archive's S3 address and a read-only key, from whoever runs DbnSync
    (<a href="docs/dbnsync#keys">how they make one</a>). Log in once; <code>dbnf</code> checks the key reaches the
    archive before saving it, and asks for the secret so it never lands in your shell history.
</p>
<CodeBlock Title="Any terminal" Text="@Login" />
<ul>
    <li><strong>Where it is kept:</strong> in <code>%APPDATA%\DbnFetch</code> on Windows, with the secret encrypted for your Windows user. On Linux it is in <code>~/.config/DbnFetch</code> and on macOS in <code>~/Library/Application Support/DbnFetch</code>, readable only by you.</li>
    <li><strong>Check everything:</strong> <code>dbnf doctor</code> tests the login, the archive, your clock (S3 refuses requests from a clock more than 15 minutes out) and the cache, which fails the check with under 10 GB free on its drive.</li>
    <li><strong>Another bucket:</strong> <code>login --bucket</code> names a bucket other than <code>dbn</code>, and <code>--prefix</code> a folder inside it that holds the archive.</li>
    <li><strong>More than one archive:</strong> add <code>--profile work</code> to <code>login</code> and to every later command.</li>
    <li><strong>Scripts and CI:</strong> set <code>DBNF_LOCATION</code>, <code>DBNF_ACCESS_KEY</code> and <code>DBNF_SECRET_KEY</code> (and <code>DBNF_BUCKET</code> if it isn't <code>dbn</code>) instead of logging in, or pipe the secret with <code>--secret-stdin</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>A folder instead of S3:</strong> <code>--archive D:\dbn</code> on any command reads a folder in the archive's layout, such as one <code>dbnf pull --out</code> filled. No login needed.</li>
    <li><strong>Forget it:</strong> <code>dbnf logout</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="explore">See what the archive holds</h2>
<p><code>dbnf ls</code> lists every series; <code>--root</code> and <code>--schema</code> narrow it.</p>
<CodeBlock Title="dbnf ls --root ES" Text="@LsOutput" />
<ul>
    <li><code>dbnf coverage ES</code> draws a root day by day, a month to a line: stored, no data, or missing. <code>--schema</code>, <code>--months 24</code> or <code>--all</code> change what it draws.</li>
    <li><code>dbnf contracts ES</code> lists the root's contracts with expiry and tick size; <code>--active</code> keeps the unexpired, and <code>--spreads</code> adds spreads. Contracts show with two-digit years (<code>ESZ26</code>); CME's own <code>ESZ6</code> is accepted too.</li>
    <li><code>dbnf show</code> prints a day's records, in Chicago time unless <code>--tz</code> says otherwise. The symbol can be a root, a contract or, for <code>mbo</code>, <code>MES.n.0</code> or <code>MES.n.1</code>.</li>
</ul>
<CodeBlock Title="dbnf show mbo MESZ26 2026-10-30 --limit 3" Text="@ShowOutput" />
<p>Each day's <code>mbo</code> opens with a snapshot of the book: a clear, then every resting order.</p>

<h2 id="pull">Download raw files</h2>
<p><code>dbnf pull</code> downloads <code>.dbn.zst</code> files exactly as stored, one UTC day each, for one or more schemas and roots.</p>
<CodeBlock Title="Any terminal" Text="@Pull" />
<ul>
    <li>Without <code>--out</code>, files go to the <a href="docs/dbnfetch#cache">cache</a>. With it, they land in the archive's own layout under that folder; <code>--flat</code> puts them all in the folder itself, and <code>--markers</code> also writes the <code>.empty</code> files for days with no data.</li>
    <li>Files already there are skipped, unless <code>--force</code>. An interrupted download carries on where it stopped.</li>
    <li><code>--dry-run</code> shows what would come down and how many GB. <code>--parallel</code> sets how many files download at once (4).</li>
    <li>Days: <code>--from</code> and <code>--to</code>, <code>--last 30d</code> (or <code>8w</code>, <code>6m</code>, <code>1y</code>), or <code>--since</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="export">Export processed data</h2>
<p>
    <code>dbnf export &lt;schema&gt; &lt;symbol&gt;</code> reads the files it needs (through the cache), and writes
    records or bars for CME trade dates. Tuesday's trade date runs from Monday 17:00 to Tuesday 16:00 Chicago time,
    and it reads the UTC days that covers. Trading stops at 16:00, but settlement and status messages land in the
    hour after it, so records stamped 16:00 to 17:00 count to the trade date that just closed.
</p>
<h3 id="symbols">Symbols</h3>
<div class="table">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>ES</code></td><td>Every contract of the root, each row naming its contract. <code>--no-spreads</code> leaves spreads out.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ESZ26</code></td><td>One contract.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ES.c.0</code></td><td>Continuous, rolling by the calendar: <code>--roll-days</code> (8) before expiry.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ES.v.0</code></td><td>Continuous, rolling to the contract with the most volume in the previous session.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ES.n.0</code>, <code>ES.n.1</code></td><td>Continuous by open interest; <code>.1</code> is the second contract. The only continuous names <code>mbo</code> holds.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p>
    A roll is decided from the last complete session and takes effect at the next open. The schedule starts a week
    before the first date asked for, so a date holds the same contract whatever span it is asked in. <code>--adjust difference</code>
    adds each roll's settlement gap to every earlier price (back-adjustment); <code>--adjust ratio</code> scales them,
    keeping percentage moves. Adjustment applies to bars: order book events keep raw prices, so <code>--adjust</code>
    on an <code>mbo</code> series is refused. A roll with no settlement for either contract has no gap, so an adjusted
    export that crosses it is refused, naming the roll and the contracts; export it unadjusted, or add that session's
    <code>statistics</code> to the archive.
</p>
<h3 id="hours">Hours</h3>
<p>
    <code>--session eth</code> (the default) is the whole Globex session, with the hour after the close where settlement
    and status messages land; <code>--session rth</code> is the root's
    regular hours; or give your own with <code>--hours 09:30-16:00 --hours-tz America/New_York</code>. Times are written
    in Chicago unless <code>--tz</code> names another zone, such as <code>UTC</code> or <code>America/New_York</code>.
</p>
<h3 id="bars">Bars</h3>
<p>
    <code>--bars</code> makes bars instead of records: <code>5s</code>, <code>1m</code>, <code>15m</code>,
    <code>1h</code>, <code>1d</code>, <code>1w</code> or <code>1mo</code> from <code>ohlcv-1s</code>, or
    <code>500t</code> (every 500 trades) and <code>1000v</code> (every 1,000 contracts) from <code>mbo</code>. Bars count
    from the session's open, so a 15-minute RTH bar starts at 08:30, 08:45 and so on. A bar belongs to one contract, so
    a <code>1w</code> or <code>1mo</code> bar of a continuous series splits at a roll: one bar per contract.
</p>
<h3 id="from-mbo">Order books from mbo</h3>
<p>
    <code>--as</code> rebuilds the order book from <code>mbo</code> and writes a schema Databento® sells separately:
    <code>trades</code>, <code>tbbo</code>, <code>mbp-1</code>, <code>mbp-10</code>, <code>bbo-1s</code> or
    <code>bbo-1m</code>. The trades of a day add up to the archive's own <code>ohlcv-1s</code> volume, and the rebuilt
    book is never crossed. Use a contract or <code>.n.0</code>/<code>.n.1</code>.
</p>
<h3 id="formats">Formats and files</h3>
<ul>
    <li><code>--format</code>: <code>csv</code> (default), <code>parquet</code>, <code>jsonl</code>, <code>dbn</code> (a filtered copy of the records, for Databento's own tools) or <code>nt8</code>. <code>--compress gzip</code> or <code>zstd</code> for <code>csv</code>, <code>jsonl</code> and <code>dbn</code>; Parquet is always compressed inside, and NinjaTrader reads plain text.</li>
    <li><strong>Columns</strong> use Databento's field names; <code>symbol</code> names the contract and <code>series</code> the continuous name. <code>--columns ts_event,close</code> keeps some, in that order.</li>
    <li><strong>Units:</strong> <code>--price ticks</code> or <code>raw</code> (Databento's integers, 1e-9) instead of decimals; <code>--time unix-ns</code>, <code>unix-ms</code> or <code>excel</code> instead of ISO 8601. <code>--split-time</code> writes an ISO time as a date and a time, <code>ts_event_date</code> and <code>ts_event_time</code>, as columns in CSV and fields in JSON Lines.</li>
    <li><strong>Files:</strong> one per trade date by default; <code>--single</code> for one file; <code>--partition</code> for <code>series=</code>/<code>date=</code> folders that DuckDB and Polars read as one table; <code>--name</code> with <code>{series}</code>, <code>{kind}</code>, <code>{date}</code>, <code>{from}</code>, <code>{to}</code> and <code>{ext}</code> for names of your own. A name without <code>{date}</code> writes one file for the whole export. <code>--out</code> picks the folder.</li>
</ul>

