<h2 id="platforms">Into trading platforms</h2>
<p>
    Trading platforms such as NinjaTrader, MetaTrader and TradeStation each import history in their own way. DbnFetch
    writes one platform's format natively, NinjaTrader 8's, and plain CSV for the rest. It does not write
    MetaTrader's or TradeStation's formats, and their import steps are not covered here.
</p>
<h3 id="ninjatrader">NinjaTrader 8</h3>
<p>
    <code>--format nt8</code> writes NinjaTrader's import text: bars from <code>--bars</code>, or ticks from
    <code>--as trades</code>. Bars are stamped with their end; a bar of a day or longer has the date alone, whatever the
    session. A contract's file is named for NinjaTrader's instrument, such as <code>ES 12-26.Last.txt</code>, which is
    how NinjaTrader knows where to put it. NinjaTrader's layout is fixed, so <code>--columns</code> is refused.
</p>
<CodeBlock Title="Any terminal" Text="@Nt8" />
<ol class="steps-list">
    <li>In NinjaTrader 8: <strong>Control Center → Tools → Import → Historical Data</strong>.</li>
    <li>Choose the format with <strong>end of bar</strong> timestamps, and set the time zone to the one you exported in (Chicago unless you passed <code>--tz</code>).</li>
    <li>Add the file and import. For a continuous series, rename the file to the instrument you want it in first.</li>
</ol>
<h3 id="other-platforms">Other platforms</h3>
<p>
    Export bars as CSV, keep only the columns your platform's importer asks for, in its order, and write times in the
    zone it expects. Then follow the platform's own import instructions.
</p>
<CodeBlock Title="Any terminal" Text="@PlatformCsv" />

<h2 id="rolls">Rolls</h2>
<p>
    <code>dbnf rolls</code> lists a continuous series' rolls: the first trade date the new contract is used, the
    contracts, and the settlement gap that <code>--adjust</code> uses. A roll decided by Friday's volume shows Monday,
    since Sunday's open belongs to Monday's trade date. Exchange holidays are not known, so one is not skipped. A roll
    with no settlement shows dashes.
</p>
<CodeBlock Title="Any terminal" Text="dbnf rolls ES.v.0 --since 2025-01-01" />

<h2 id="tapes">Tapes</h2>
<p>
    <code>dbnf tape</code> builds compact files for backtesting from the archive, and proves them against it. See
    <a href="docs/dbntape">DbnTape</a>.
</p>
<CodeBlock Title="Any terminal" Text="dbnf tape build ES --tapes D:\Tapes --from 2026-10-29" />

<h2 id="cache">The cache</h2>
<p>
    Raw files are cached in <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\DbnFetch\cache</code> on Windows and
    <code>~/.cache/DbnFetch</code> elsewhere, in a folder for each login, so a second export of the same days downloads nothing. A file that
    changes in the archive is downloaded again. <code>--cache</code> on any command uses another folder.
</p>
<ul>
    <li><code>dbnf cache</code> shows what it holds.</li>
    <li><code>dbnf cache clear</code> deletes all of it; <code>dbnf cache clear mbo</code> one schema; <code>dbnf cache clear mbo MNQ</code> one schema of one root.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    A cached file that is damaged stops the command with an error naming it and the command that mends it, such as
    <code>dbnf pull mbo MNQ --from 2026-10-29 --force</code>, which downloads it again. In a folder archive, copy a good
    file over it. If <code>profiles.json</code> or <code>secrets.json</code> is damaged, the error names it; run
    <code>dbnf login</code> again, which replaces it and keeps the old file beside it, ending <code>.damaged</code>.
</p>

<h2 id="exit">Exit codes</h2>
<div class="table">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>It worked.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>The work failed: the archive refused or couldn't be reached, a file is damaged, a roll has no settlement. <code>dbnf doctor</code> also exits 1 if a check fails.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>2</code></td><td>A usage mistake: an option or argument dbnf can't parse, a value it can't use, a request it refuses, or no login.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>130</code></td><td>Stopped with Ctrl+C, in a command that reads the archive.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h2 id="reference">Option reference</h2>
<p><code>dbnf --help</code> and <code>dbnf &lt;command&gt; --help</code> print every option. These work on every command:</p>
<div class="table">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>--profile</code></td><td><code>default</code></td><td>Which saved login to use.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--archive</code></td><td>the login's</td><td>Read a folder instead.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--cache</code></td><td>see above</td><td>Where raw files are kept once downloaded.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<div class="table">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>login</code>, <code>logout</code></td><td>Save or forget the archive's address and your key.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>doctor</code></td><td>Check the login, the archive, the clock and the cache.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ls</code></td><td>List series: schema, root, days, size, first and last day.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>coverage &lt;root&gt;</code></td><td>One root day by day.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>contracts &lt;root&gt;</code></td><td>Contracts with expiry and tick size.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>show &lt;schema&gt; &lt;symbol&gt; &lt;day&gt;</code></td><td>Print one UTC day's records.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>pull &lt;schemas&gt; &lt;roots&gt;</code></td><td>Download raw files.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>export &lt;schema&gt; &lt;symbol&gt;</code></td><td>Write records or bars for trade dates.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>rolls &lt;series&gt;</code></td><td>List a continuous series' rolls.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>tape</code></td><td>Build, inspect and prove tapes: <code>catalog</code>, <code>build</code>, <code>verify</code>, <code>pull</code>, <code>info</code>, <code>schema</code>, <code>bench</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>cache</code>, <code>cache clear</code></td><td>Show or clear the local cache.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

